        "math_academy_id": "q-74407",
        "problem": "$\\int (3{x}^{2}+10{sin}^{2}\u20615x-10{cos}^{2}\u20615x)\\text{d}x=$",
        "worked_solution": "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\u20612\\theta ={cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta -{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta \n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =5x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\u206110x={cos}^{2}\u20615x-{sin}^{2}\u20615x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3{x}^{2}+10{sin}^{2}\u20615x-10{cos}^{2}\u20615x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (3{x}^{2}-10({cos}^{2}\u20615x-{sin}^{2}\u20615x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (3{x}^{2}-10\\operatorname{cos}\u206110x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-10(\\frac{1}{10}\\,\\operatorname{sin}\u206110x)+C \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-\\operatorname{sin}\u206110x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "actual_result": "Correct",
